{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian inference starts from a known probability distribution of observations $X$ parameterized by a parameter $\\theta$ (written as $P(X | \\theta)$).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:13\u201300:32:53] And here comes an alternative Bayesian inference. And Bayesian inference, so we need to start from, again, I mentioned that we still have n observations that come from distribution of X parameterized by a non-value parameter theta. We can also rewrite it as false as Bayesians usually do, P of X given theta. This is actually the same thing."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Prior distribution reflects initial knowledge/preference about possible values of $\\theta$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:53\u201300:33:37] We start from prior distribution that reflects our knowledge, our preference, and possible values of theta."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Posterior distribution is derived by applying Bayes' theorem to update prior knowledge with observed data $X_1, ..., X_n$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:32:53\u201300:33:37] So we simply derive a posterior distribution, P of theta given X1, Xn, by applying Bayes' theorem."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian method yields a distribution (uncertainty about $\\theta$) versus frequentist point estimate.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:34:14\u201300:34:56] Unlike maximum life estimation, our answer is no longer a point estimate, but a distribution."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is given by $P(\\theta | X) = \\frac{P(X | \\theta) \\cdot P(\\theta)}{P(X)}$ where $P(X)$ is the marginal likelihood.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:33:37\u201300:34:13] So like if a function looks like this, so this is just a product of P of Xi given theta, since all of our observations are independent and identically distributed, we multiply it by prior distribution and we renormalize it according to Bayes' theorem."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The posterior distribution is a product of likelihood $P(X_i | \\theta)$ and prior $P(\\theta)$, renormalized by $P(X)$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:33:37\u201300:34:13] So like if a function looks like this, so this is just a product of P of Xi given theta, since all of our observations are independent and identically distributed, we multiply it by prior distribution and we renormalize it according to Bayes' theorem."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The phrase 'maximum life estimation' is a mishearing of 'maximum likelihood estimation' (MLE).",
      "supported": false,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:34:14\u201300:34:56] Unlike maximum life estimation, our answer is no longer a point estimate, but a distribution. This is flagged as uncertain in the notes, but the ASR itself does not explicitly state what 'maximum life estimation' is supposed to be; it's a mishearing that the notes flag as uncertain."
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 4,
  "caveat": "The ASR contains a possible mishearing of 'maximum likelihood estimation' as 'maximum life estimation', which is flagged as uncertain in the notes but not explicitly confirmed by the ASR."
}
